body:
**Is it the case that Form-Associated Custom Element authors must consider _two_ values — the `value` IDL attribute and some additional `submissionValue` IDL attribute?**

The language around _values_ for form controls is fairly loaded in the specification:

* The "value" [content attribute](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#attr-input-value)
* The "value" [IDL attribute](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#dom-input-value):
  * In the [value](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#dom-input-value-value) mode
  * In the [default](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#dom-input-value-default) mode
  * In the [default/on](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#dom-input-value-default-on) mode
  * In the [filename](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#dom-input-value-filename) mode
* The [submission value](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#face-submission-value) for a [Form-Associated Custom Element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#form-associated-custom-elements)
* And the description of [setFormValue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#dom-elementinternals-setformvalue) in the [ElementInternals](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/custom-elements.html#element-internals) specification
* Related, the nuanced logic around [constructing the entry list](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#constructing-form-data-set)

Potential differences in implementation around "value" vs "submission value" would change how custom form control interfaces are designed. Consider the following two options (with only relevant lines included).

```
// If "value === submissionValue", concessions must be made
//  to allow conditional participation in form submission.
class FancyCheckbox extends HTMLElement {
  get checked() { return this.__checked; }
  set checked(value) { this.__checked = value; }
  get fancyValue() { return this.getAttribute('value') ?? 'on'; }
  set fancyValue(value) { this.setAttribute('value', value); }
  get value() { return this.checked ? this.fancyValue : null; }
  set value(value) { /* read only */ }
}
```

```
// ... But, if "value !== submissionValue" a more conventional interface
// could still be exposed.
class FancyCheckbox extends HTMLElement {
  get checked() { return this.__checked; }
  set checked(value) { this.__checked = value; }
  get value() { return this.getAttribute('value') ?? 'on'; }
  set value(value) { this.setAttribute('value', value); }
  get submissionValue() { return this.checked ? this.value : null; }
  set submissionValue(value) { /* read only */ }
}
```

To be clear, I'm not necessarily proposing anything, just looking for clarification! Of course, thanks for all the hard work — I'm incredibly excited to start using Form-Associated Custom Elements.
